# (script line, picture description) — one line = one scene; 4 scenes = 1 grid
T="Tunde"; A="Auntie Ngozi"
S = [
# COLD OPEN
("It is six in the morning in Lagos. Your alarm rings, and you reach out without opening your eyes.", f"{T} asleep in bed under a bright window, one arm stretched out to a small glowing phone that is ringing, musical notes around it."),
("Before you even stand up, you check your messages, the weather and the football scores.", f"{T} sitting up in bed, face lit by the phone, little icons floating above it: a cloud, a football, a speech bubble."),
("You use this thing many times a day, and you hardly think about it. But look at it for a second.", f"A giant close-up of a modern smartphone held in a hand, shiny glass front, glowing screen, sparkles around it."),
("It talks to people on the other side of the world. It is a camera, a map, a bank and a torch.", f"The phone floating in the middle, with small icons flying out around it: a camera, a map, a naira note, a torch beam."),
("And here is the strange part. Everything inside it was dug out of the ground. Literally.", f"{A} holding the phone up to her eye like a detective, shocked, while a cartoon shovel and dirt hill sit behind her."),
("Reportedly, more than sixty different elements are packed in there. Metals, sand, even the glass of the screen.", f"The phone split open like a cartoon cutaway, with a pile of tiny coloured rocks, sand and metal bits spilling out from it."),
("So how did humans invent the cell phone? Let's find out.", f"{T} and {A} standing in front of a giant glowing phone, both looking amazed, a big question mark above them."),
# CHAPTER 1 THE METALS
("CHAPTER 1: THE METALS. Let's start with the oldest ingredient, copper.", f"A chapter-number scene: a big shiny copper-coloured lump on a table, {T} holding a pencil and notebook, looking curious."),
("Around seven thousand years ago, people began melting copper out of rocks. It was one of the first metals humans learned to work.", "An ancient cartoon workshop with a fire pit and clay oven, a worker pouring glowing orange metal into a stone mould."),
("Copper carries electricity better than almost any cheap metal. So today it runs through every wire in your phone.", f"A close-up of a tiny orange wire winding through a phone circuit, a yellow spark glowing along it, {T} pointing at it."),
("Then there is gold. It is soft and it never rusts, so a thin layer protects the tiny connectors inside.", "A tiny gold-coloured connector plug glowing beside a small pile of gold bars, a magnifying glass over it."),
("Mix copper with tin, and you get bronze. That helped start a whole new age, about five thousand years ago.", "An ancient bronze sword and shield on a table next to a lump of tin and a lump of copper, a smiling blacksmith."),
("Tin did not go away. Even now, a little tin is used to join the parts inside your phone.", f"A cartoon soldering iron joining tiny parts on a green circuit board, a small silver blob of metal at its tip, {A} watching."),
("Remember copper and tin. They will come back.", f"{T} and {A} with a notebook that has a copper lump and a tin lump drawn on it, both pointing at it and winking."),
# CHAPTER 2 SAND AND GLASS
("CHAPTER 2: SAND AND GLASS. Now picture a beach.", "A sunny beach with golden sand dunes, a few palm trees, and a giant chapter-two sand castle in the shape of a phone."),
("About three and a half thousand years ago, craftspeople in the Middle East and Egypt learned to melt sand into glass.", "Ancient craftspeople heating sand in a clay furnace, glowing orange glass dripping into a mould, a pyramid in the distance."),
("Early glass was cloudy and rare. But they found that adding a little cobalt turned it a deep, beautiful blue.", "A rich cobalt-blue glass bottle on a table next to a pile of blue powder, an ancient craftsperson smiling proudly."),
("Remember cobalt. That blue mineral has a big role to play in your phone.", f"{A} holding a blue chunk of mineral up to the light, {T} looking amazed beside her."),
("Over the centuries, glass got flatter, clearer and stronger, until it could cover a screen.", "A row of glass objects from left to right, getting clearer and flatter: a bottle, a window pane, a phone screen."),
("Hold that thought. We will come back to the screen. First, we need a spark.", f"{T} holding a small glowing spark between his fingers, {A} leaning in to look, dark background."),
# CHAPTER 3 SPARK AND TELEPHONE
("CHAPTER 3: THE SPARK. Long before phones, people noticed odd things in nature.", "A sunny hillside with an old Greek-style scholar watching a rubbed piece of amber lift a tiny feather."),
("A rock called lodestone pulled iron towards it, and rubbed amber made small feathers jump.", "A lodestone rock pulling nails towards it, and an amber stone lifting feathers beside it."),
("Nobody knew why. It looked like magic. But those clues pointed to a hidden force: electricity.", f"{T} scratching his head at a table of odd stones, a lightning bolt glowing in the sky outside the window."),
("In 1800, an Italian scientist called Alessandro Volta stacked metal discs and made the first battery.", "A cartoon scientist standing next to a tall stack of coin-like metal discs with a wire, a bright bulb glowing in the background."),
("For the first time, humans had a steady flow of electricity they could control.", "A small bulb glowing on a table, wires leading to a simple battery stack, cheerful sparks flying."),
("Then came the telegraph. Dots and dashes could race along a wire, and messages crossed countries in seconds.", "A telegraph operator tapping a key, a long wire stretching on poles over green hills toward a distant town."),
("In 1876, Alexander Graham Bell patented a way to send actual voices down a wire. The telephone was born.", "A cartoon inventor beside an early wooden telephone box, his assistant holding an earpiece, a long wire running out the window."),
("The first words are reported to have been something like, 'Mr. Watson, come here. I want to see you.'", f"A workshop scene: one man shouting into a funnel, the other man jumping up with an earpiece, {A} laughing in the corner."),
("But there was a problem. A voice could only travel where a wire was already laid.", f"{T} holding a tangled mess of wires attached to a phone, going nowhere, wires everywhere around the room."),
# CHAPTER 4 INVISIBLE WAVES
("CHAPTER 4: INVISIBLE WAVES. What if the message could fly without a wire?", "A glowing wave rising out of an antenna into the sky, with little music notes riding the wave."),
("In the 1860s, a Scottish physicist, James Clerk Maxwell, worked out on paper that light and electricity are connected, and that invisible electric waves must exist.", "A scholar in an old study writing equations on paper while an invisible wave drawn as a glowing ribbon floats out the window."),
("In 1887, a German scientist, Heinrich Hertz, proved it. He sent a spark across a room and caught it with a small loop of wire.", "A cartoon laboratory: a spark jumping between two metal balls on one side of the table, and a small loop of wire sparking back on the other."),
("Those invisible waves are what we now call radio waves. Hertz even gave his name to the way we measure them.", f"{T} holding a radio and {A} dancing beside it, little wave lines coming out of the speaker."),
("Then Guglielmo Marconi turned them into a message machine. By 1901, he reported a signal sent across the Atlantic Ocean.", "A big antenna on a cliff beside the ocean, a glowing wave line crossing the sea to a far-away shore."),
("Now voice could travel with no wire at all. But radios were big, and they only worked one way at a time.", f"{T} lugging a huge radio on his back, sweating, while {A} giggles beside him."),
# CHAPTER 5 THE BRAIN
("CHAPTER 5: THE BRAIN. A phone needs more than a voice. It needs a brain.", f"{T} and {A} looking at a cartoon brain that is made of circuit lines, glowing blue."),
("Early electronics used glass tubes called vacuum tubes. They were big, hot and burned out all the time.", "A giant room filled with huge glowing vacuum tubes and cables, a worker with a trolley replacing a burnt-out tube."),
("Then, in 1947, three scientists at Bell Labs, John Bardeen, Walter Brattain and William Shockley, built the transistor.", "Three cartoon scientists in a lab with a tiny metal-and-wire gadget glowing on the table, all hands clapping."),
("A transistor is a tiny switch with no moving parts. Turn it on, turn it off. That is the language of every computer.", "A single glowing switch flipping on and off, with a large 1 and 0 style on/off light bulb pair beside it. No letters."),
("Even better, it could be made from silicon, and silicon is made from ordinary sand.", f"A pile of beach sand beside a shiny silver silicon disc, {T} holding the disc up in wonder."),
("So the beach from chapter two came back. Sand, cleaned and melted into near-perfect crystal.", "A giant furnace pulling a long shining crystal rod out of molten glow, workers in helmets looking up at it."),
("In the late 1950s, Jack Kilby and Robert Noyce found how to put many transistors on one chip.", "A tiny square chip with a handful of circuits shown under a microscope, two engineers high-fiving."),
("Today, a chip the size of a fingernail can hold billions of transistors. That is the brain in your pocket.", f"A fingernail with a tiny chip on it, magnified in a large magnifying glass, {A} gasping."),
# CHAPTER 6 THE CELL
("CHAPTER 6: THE CELL. Now we have a voice, a wire-free wave, and a brain. How do we connect millions of people at once?", "A simple map of a city with little people holding phones and no towers yet, question marks everywhere."),
("Early car phones used one giant tower for a whole city, so only a few people could talk at once.", "One tall tower in a big city, with only three tiny car phones connected and a long queue of waiting cars."),
("Then Bell Labs engineers had an idea in the late 1940s: split the city into small areas called cells, each with its own tower.", "A city map divided into colourful hexagon cells, each hexagon with a small tower in the middle."),
("As you move, your call is passed from one cell to the next, like a baton in a relay race.", f"{T} walking along a road with a phone, a relay baton glowing as it passes from tower to tower."),
("Each cell can reuse the same radio channels as far-away cells. That is how millions of people can talk at the same time.", "A map of many hexagon cells with the same colours repeating, tiny callers in each one, all happy."),
("Then on 3 April 1973, a Motorola engineer called Martin Cooper stood on a New York street and made the first public call from a handheld mobile phone.", "A man in a 1970s suit standing on a New York street holding a huge brick-like phone to his ear, taxi cabs behind him."),
("According to the story he tells, he called a rival engineer at Bell Labs to say, 'I'm calling you from a real cell phone.'", f"A man on a street with a brick phone grinning, and a rival engineer in an office holding the phone, jaw dropped. {A} peeking from a corner."),
("That first phone weighed about a kilogram. You could talk for roughly half an hour, then charge it for about ten hours.", f"{T} straining to hold a huge brick-like phone with both hands, a tiny battery meter on its side, {A} laughing."),
("In 1983, the first commercial version went on sale for about four thousand dollars. Only the rich could own one.", "A fancy shop window with a single brick phone on a velvet cushion, a rich man in a suit buying it with a pile of cash."),
# CHAPTER 7 POWER AND SCREEN
("CHAPTER 7: POWER AND GLASS. A phone that dies in an hour is not much use. It needed a better battery.", f"{T} staring at a phone with an empty-battery light, a tiny cloud of sadness above him."),
("In the 1970s, British chemist Stanley Whittingham found a way to store energy in lithium. John Goodenough later made it far more powerful.", "A cartoon scientist holding a glowing battery with a cluster of lithium atoms, another scientist beside him adding a blue mineral piece."),
("And that blue mineral from the glass maker? Cobalt. It was a key part of the new battery.", f"A blue cobalt chunk dropping into a battery with a glowing lightning bolt, {A} pointing and shouting in delight."),
("In 1991, Sony brought the lithium-ion battery to market, and Akira Yoshino's safer design helped make it practical.", "A neat row of small batteries on a conveyor belt in a factory, a happy worker in a hard hat giving a thumbs up."),
("But there is a cost. Much of the world's cobalt is mined in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, and there are serious reports of dangerous work and child labour.", f"A mine with workers carrying sacks of blue rocks, {T} and {A} looking at it with concerned faces, a worried sun above."),
("That is why many companies are now working on tracing cobalt and on batteries that use less of it.", "A lab scene with scientists testing a new battery type, a tracking tag on a blue mineral sack, a green recycling arrow."),
("Now the screen. The glass is often strengthened by dipping it in a hot bath of potassium salt, so the surface can resist cracks.", "A sheet of glass being lowered into a glowing bath of hot liquid in a factory vat, steam rising."),
("Behind the glass sits a display. In 1888, an Austrian botanist, Friedrich Reinitzer, noticed a carrot chemical that melted in two stages and turned cloudy before turning clear.", "A cartoon botanist squinting at a test tube with an orange liquid, cloudy and clear at the same time, a carrot on the table."),
("That strange state is a liquid crystal. Control it with electricity, and you can control light.", "A tiny stack of pixels on a screen switching colour as electric sparks pass through, rainbow glow."),
("Finally, your fingers. A clear coating made from indium and tin senses your touch, because your finger carries a tiny electric charge.", f"{T} touching a glowing screen with his finger, ripples of electric blue spreading from the touch. {A} laughs."),
("And there is the tin again, just as I promised.", f"{A} holding up the notebook with the tin lump drawn on it, grinning and checking it off."),
# CHAPTER 8 LEGACY / NIGERIA
("CHAPTER 8: THE POCKET. Put it all together and you get a smartphone.", "All the parts floating together: copper wire, gold plug, sand chip, blue cobalt battery, glass screen, finger touch, glowing as one phone."),
("The IBM Simon, in the early 1990s, was an early attempt. In 2007, a touchscreen phone changed the game for everyone.", "Two phones side by side: a chunky grey early touchscreen phone on the left, a sleek modern phone on the right, a giant arrow between them."),
("Now think about Nigeria. Before 2001, there were very few phone lines, and reportedly fewer than half a million working ones for a very large country.", "A Lagos street with a lonely public phone box, a long queue of people waiting in the sun, a small number of wire poles."),
("In January 2001, Nigeria held an auction for mobile licences. By August 2001, GSM service had started.", f"A cartoon auction hall with bidders holding up paddles, and a happy official stamping a licence. {T} watching from the side."),
("Since then, mobile phones have spread across the country. Today, well over a hundred million lines are reported in use.", "A bright aerial view of Nigeria with glowing dots lighting up the whole map, towers and phone icons everywhere."),
("For millions of people, the phone became their bank, their shop, their classroom and their link to family far away.", f"A busy street collage in one picture: a trader taking mobile payments, a student watching a lesson, {A} on a video call with a relative."),
("Think of the chain. Rocks from the ground. Sand from the beach. Waves in the air. Thousands of minds, over thousands of years.", "A long glowing chain across the picture: ore, sand, wires, waves, chips, battery, screen, ending at a hand holding a phone."),
("Back to your alarm. That little rectangle was not invented by one person on one day.", f"{T} waking in bed again with the phone alarm, but now smiling and looking at it with new respect. Morning light."),
("It is a pocket full of human history, dug up, melted, grown from sand and stitched together by people you will never meet.", f"{T} and {A} standing in a sunlit street, each holding up a glowing phone to the sky, a flock of birds flying by."),
("And that is the story of the cell phone.", f"{T} and {A} waving at the camera next to a giant phone shape, golden light."),
("Next time, we trace another thing in your pocket: the small piece of plastic that lets you pay without cash. How did humans invent the credit card?", f"{T} pulling a shiny bank card out of his wallet, {A} pointing at it with wide eyes, a big arrow glowing toward the card."),
("If this surprised you, tell us in the comments which part you did not know, and what we should trace next.", f"{T} and {A} holding up a big speech bubble, standing before a glowing subscribe bell shape and thumbs up icon. No text."),
]
